      Constructors
----------------------
  Defination -  A Constructor is a special member of a class  used to initialize an object and it is automatically invoked when an object is created..
 
 Q)why only a constructor?
    They provide an efficient way  for initializing instance variables by passing values by invoking a constructor.

Internal Process of Object Creation & a Constructor call
---------------------------------------------------------
      Student s1 = new Student();

    new Student() // new keyword used to create object in the heap memory 
      (step 1 - JVM allocates memory for the object in the heap,
       step 2 - Objects instance variables are assigned with default values.)

        ↓

    Student() constructor executes
    (step 3 - constructor gets invoked to assign object values,
     step 4 - Object gets initialized with its valess.)
      
      ↓

     s1 // reference variable
     (step 5 - s1 store a copy of obj values as reference in stack memory.)

Types of constructor
----------------------

1) Default Constructor -> when no constructor explicitly defined in the program, then when an 
object created and calls a no parameterised constructor then java compiler internally creates a default constructor and 
assign default values based on  type of variables.

 Student() {
    super(); // Java's compiler provides a default no-argument constructor.
}


2) parameterised Constructor -> A constructor that accepts parameters.

Ex - class Student {
    String name;
    int rollno;

    Student(String name, int rollno) {
        this.name = name
        this.rollno = rollno ;
    }
}

class Main {
  public static void main(String args[]){
    Student s1 = new Student("mmb",87);
  }
}
 
